<Base title="From equipment signals to useful context — Evan Han"><article class="article"><a class="back" href="/#notes">← Back to field notes</a><div class="eyebrow">SYSTEMS / PLC / MES</div><h1>From equipment signals to useful context</h1><p class="notice">A conceptual example for learning; it does not describe a real production line.</p><p>A single equipment signal can indicate that something happened. Production software often needs more context: which asset generated the event, when it occurred, and whether the information is current.</p><h2>Build a shared vocabulary</h2><p>Start by documenting signal meanings, units, identifiers, and state transitions. Distinguish a raw PLC value from an interpreted equipment state and from a production record in an MES.</p><h2>Plan for imperfect delivery</h2><p>Connections can drop and clocks can differ. Consider timestamps, duplicates, ordering, data quality and recovery before deciding how an event will be used. The appropriate guarantees depend on the system and its operational requirements.</p><h2>Make assumptions visible</h2><p>Use simulated equipment and sample events to walk through normal and exceptional cases with stakeholders. Clear boundaries and observable behavior make integration easier to reason about.</p></article></Base>

